# Second occurrence (CI run 33149911511, job 98779260996, commit 7dba784f)
#
# Identical shape: reactor = io_uring, "shard 0 closing conn 2: query buffer
# exceeded 4096 bytes" present, client's 64-iteration loop (~4 s) saw neither
# EOF nor a write error. The first entry above said "not reproduced"; it has
# now reproduced, and that sentence is superseded rather than edited, because
# what it recorded was true when written.
#
# Rate: 2 failures against 7 successes across the completed runs of
# `test (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)` on this branch (9 more were cancelled by
# pushes). Roughly one run in five.
#
# What the second look established, and what it did not:
#
#   uring_reactor.rs:316   reap_counter = reap_counter.wrapping_add(1);
#                          if reap_counter & 0xF == 0 { uring_reap_closed(..) }
#
# The fd is not dropped by `uring_mark_closing` — that sets a flag, queues the
# conn for the arm loop and pushes it to a ready-set. `uring_reap_closed` does
# the drop, and it runs on every SIXTEENTH reactor iteration. So a closing
# conn's teardown is gated behind a counter, not behind its own readiness.
#
# That is worth knowing and does NOT explain four seconds. `uring_park` blocks
# in submit_and_wait(1) with a bounding timeout of 50 ms (tick cadence), so
# iterations continue even on an idle shard: sixteen of them is 800 ms worst
# case, and the test's window is about 4 s. The client also writes every ~62 ms,
# and each write that is still being received produces a CQE.
#
# So: the gate is real, the arithmetic does not close, and the remaining gap is
# unexplained. Not reproducible here — macOS has no io_uring — and every commit
# in flight when both failures happened touched docs, gate scripts and
# allocation bounds, nothing in crates/kevy, kevy-rt or uring.
